{
  "image_name": "7d002ec51c122810a93a99f783910fb8.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how different halide perovskite families are thought to achieve or fail to achieve melting, and what design strategies may lower the melting temperature. It first separates perovskites by dimensionality, from 3D to 2D, and by composition: hybrid and inorganic 3D structures, then 2D structures with either one or two tethering groups. The structural cartoons use corner-sharing octahedral frameworks with different cations and organic linkers.\n\nA key message is that hybrid 3D perovskites and 2D perovskites with two tethering groups have no known melts, whereas inorganic 3D perovskites and 2D perovskites with one tethering group may be made more meltable by suppressing the melting temperature. The dashed boxes on the right show the main routes for doing this. For inorganic systems, changing electronegativity—through ionic radius or ion choice—appears to make melting mainly enthalpy-driven, or partly entropy-driven as well. For 2D systems with one tethering group, introducing chirality, adding branching, or tuning non-covalent interactions are presented as useful molecular design handles. These strategies contribute differently through enthalpy and entropy, with non-covalent interactions appearing especially entropy-driven. Overall, the figure links crystal chemistry and molecular design to melt formation in halide perovskites.",
